OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Minimal-metrics-and-interior-injectivity-for-nef-adjoints-September-27-2026", "title": "Minimal metrics and interior injectivity for nef adjoints", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Minimal-metrics-and-interior-injectivity-for-nef-adjoints-September-27-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "034", "family_title": "Log abundance for compact Kähler spaces under logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity", "abstract_html": "Let \\((H,\\Theta)\\) be a projective complex klt pair with effective rational boundary and nef ℚ-Cartier adjoint. On any projective log resolution, minimal semipositive metrics on the pulled-back adjoint exist and have zero Lelong numbers everywhere. On smooth projective complex varieties, we also prove an <i>H</i><sup>1</sup>-injectivity theorem for rational interior boundaries with simple-normal-crossing support when both endpoint bundles carry zero-Lelong semipositive metrics.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((H,\\Theta)\\) be a normal connected projective complex klt pair, with \\(\\Theta\\) an effective rational divisor.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that \\(D_H=K_H+\\Theta\\) is a nef \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier divisor.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\pi:V\\to H\\) be a projective log resolution, and let \\(N=\\pi^*D_H\\) denote the actual rational line bundle.</p><p class=\"li\">A local metric weight \\(\\varphi\\) gives squared norm \\(|\\xi|^2e^{-\\varphi}\\). Semipositivity means \\(i\\partial\\bar\\partial\\varphi\\geq0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A semipositive metric has minimal singularities if every other semipositive weight \\(\\psi\\) on the same rational line bundle satisfies \\(\\psi\\leq\\varphi+C_\\psi\\) for a constant \\(C_\\psi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Weights on rational line bundles are defined by clearing denominators and dividing by the same positive integer.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every such \\(\\pi\\), a semipositive singular Hermitian metric with minimal singularities on \\(N\\) exists, and every such metric has Lelong number zero at every point of \\(V\\). Neither \\(K_H\\) nor \\(\\Theta\\) is required to be separately \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier.</p>", "proof": "<p>The zero-dimensional case is immediate. Assume \\(n=\\dim V&gt;0\\), choose compatible canonical divisors, and write\n\\[\nN=\\pi^*(K_H+\\Theta)=K_V+B.\n\\]\nThe klt coefficients are less than one, and \\(B^-\\) is exceptional over \\(H\\). Since \\(N\\) is nef, it is pseudoeffective, so a semipositive metric \\(\\varphi\\) with minimal singularities exists by the envelope construction recalled in Proposition 2.</p><p>Suppose, to the contrary, that \\(\\varphi\\) has positive Lelong number at \\(p\\in V\\). Proposition 2 gives normalized extremal sections \\(s_m\\), an unbounded subsequence, and a fixed sublevel cutoff \\(R\\) for which\n\\[\nQ_m(s_m)=1,\\qquad s_m(p)\\neq0,\\qquad\\varepsilon_m\\to0.\n\\]</p><p>Proposition 4 gives global competitors \\(w_m\\) satisfying\n\\[\nw_m(p)=s_m(p),\n\\qquad\nQ_m(w_m)^m\\leq C_0\\varepsilon_m\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nFor large \\(m\\), therefore,\n\\[\n0&lt;Q_m(w_m)&lt;1.\n\\]\nMultiply \\(w_m\\) by \\(Q_m(w_m)^{-m/2}&gt;1\\). By homogeneity, the new section has gauge one and strictly larger absolute evaluation at \\(p\\) than \\(s_m\\). This contradicts the choice of \\(s_m\\), regardless of the rate at which \\(s_m(p)\\) or \\(\\varepsilon_m\\) tends to zero.</p><p>Hence \\(\\varphi\\) has no positive Lelong number at any point. The argument applies to every minimal semipositive metric. All constructions use the actual pulled-back adjoint bundle and require only \\(K_H+\\Theta\\) to be \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Theorem 1.2, together with its restriction consequence (1.1)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V\\) be a smooth projective complex variety, and let \\(L\\) be an integral divisor.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0\\leq C_0\\leq C_2\\) be effective rational divisors whose combined support is simple normal crossing.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that both actual rational line bundles \\(L-C_0\\) and \\(L-C_2\\) admit singular Hermitian metrics of semipositive curvature with zero Lelong numbers at every point.</p><p class=\"li\">For a rational number \\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1\\), set\n  \\[\n  C_1=(1-\\lambda)C_0+\\lambda C_2,\n  \\qquad\n  L_i=L-\\lfloor C_i\\rfloor\\quad(i=0,1).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The natural inclusion of line bundles induces an injection\n\\[\nH^1\\!\\left(V,\\mathcal O_V(K_V+L_1)\\right)\n\\longrightarrow\nH^1\\!\\left(V,\\mathcal O_V(K_V+L_0)\\right).\n\\]</p><p>Consequently, if\n\\[\nE=\\lfloor C_1\\rfloor-\\lfloor C_0\\rfloor\n\\]\nis nonzero, then \\(E\\) is an effective integral divisor, possibly nonreduced, and\n\\[\nH^0\\!\\left(V,\\mathcal O_V(K_V+L_0)\\right)\n\\longrightarrow\nH^0\\!\\left(E,\\mathcal O_V(K_V+L_0)|_E\\right)\n\\quad\\text{is surjective}.\n\\tag{1.1}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The zero-dimensional assertion is trivial. Assume \\(n=\\dim V&gt;0\\). Use the endpoint regularizations and comparison weights of Propositions 5 and 6. Put\n\\[\nD=\\operatorname{Supp}(C_0+C_2)_{\\mathrm{red}},\n\\qquad U=V\\setminus D,\n\\]\nand fix the complete metric \\(\\omega_c\\) with bounded local potentials.</p><p>Take a class in the kernel of the displayed map, and represent it by a smooth closed \\(L_1\\)-valued \\((n,1)\\)-form \\(\\gamma\\) on \\(V\\). Proposition 8 gives weighted harmonic representatives \\(u_k\\) with\n\\[\n\\gamma-u_k\\in\\overline{\\operatorname{im}\\bar\\partial}\n\\]\nin the source weighted space, uniformly bounded \\(\\|u_k\\|_{1,k}\\), and\n\\[\n\\|\\bar\\partial_{0,k}^*(su_k)\\|_{0,k}\\leq C\\sqrt{\\epsilon_k}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]</p><p>Multiplication by \\(s\\) is bounded in degrees zero and one and commutes with the distributional Dolbeault operator. Hence\n\\[\ns\\gamma-su_k\n\\]\nlies in the target closure of actual exact forms. Apply the target class map from Proposition 7. It annihilates that closure and agrees with ordinary cohomology on \\(s\\gamma\\). The latter class is zero by the choice of \\(\\gamma\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\kappa_{0,k}(su_k)=0.\n\\]</p><p>The contraction and uniform source norms bound \\(\\|su_k\\|_{0,k}\\). Proposition 7 supplies global forms \\(v_k\\) on \\(U\\) with\n\\[\n\\bar\\partial v_k=su_k,\n\\qquad\n\\sup_k\\|v_k\\|_{0,k}&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nThe adjoint-domain conclusion of Proposition 8 justifies\n\\[\n\\|su_k\\|_{0,k}^2\n=\\langle\\bar\\partial v_k,su_k\\rangle_{0,k}\n=\\langle v_k,\\bar\\partial_{0,k}^*(su_k)\\rangle_{0,k}\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{3.14}\n\\]</p><p>To retain the original ordinary class while passing to a limit, fix a smooth metric on \\(L_1\\) over all of \\(V\\), with local weight \\(h_*\\), and keep \\(\\omega_c\\) as background on \\(U\\). Uniform upper bounds in a finite framed cover give\n\\[\nh_{1,k}\\leq h_*+C_*,\n\\qquad\ne^{-h_*}\\leq e^{C_*}e^{-h_{1,k}}.\n\\tag{3.15}\n\\]\nThus each varying weighted space embeds boundedly into one fixed Hilbert space in degrees zero and one. In particular, the \\(u_k\\) have uniformly bounded fixed norms.</p><p>On every compact \\(K\\subset U\\), \\(s\\) is nowhere zero and the target weights \\(h_{0,k}\\) have uniform upper bounds. Equation (3.14) therefore implies that \\(u_k\\to0\\) in the fixed \\(L^2\\) norm on \\(K\\). This local strong convergence and the global bound give global weak convergence to zero: approximate any fixed \\(L^2\\) test form by one supported in a compact subset of \\(U\\). The compactly supported pairing tends to zero, while the norm of the remaining tail controls its pairing uniformly in \\(k\\). Concentration near \\(D\\) does not affect weak convergence.</p><p>For each \\(k\\), choose actual exact forms \\(\\bar\\partial z_{k,l}\\) converging to \\(\\gamma-u_k\\) in the source weighted norm. By (3.15), their primitives belong to the fixed degree-zero space and their derivatives converge in the fixed degree-one norm. Hence \\(\\gamma-u_k\\) belongs to the fixed closure of actual exact forms.</p><p>Apply Proposition 7 to the fixed smooth metric \\(h_*\\). Its continuous class map \\(\\kappa_*\\) annihilates that fixed closure, so\n\\[\n\\kappa_*(u_k)=\\kappa_*(\\gamma)=[\\gamma]\n\\quad\\text{for every }k.\n\\]\nThe fixed closed-form space is a closed Hilbert subspace, and its continuous linear map to finite-dimensional ordinary cohomology is weakly continuous. Since \\(u_k\\rightharpoonup0\\), the last identity forces \\([\\gamma]=0\\).</p><p>This proves analytic Dolbeault injectivity. Dolbeault theory and GAGA identify it with the natural map in algebraic coherent cohomology. The argument uses only the endpoint metrics and does not use Theorem 1.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow\\mathcal O_V(K_V+L_1)\n\\longrightarrow\\mathcal O_V(K_V+L_0)\n\\longrightarrow\\mathcal O_V(K_V+L_0)|_E\n\\longrightarrow0\n\\]\nis exact. Its connecting homomorphism has image equal to the kernel of the injective \\(H^1\\) map, hence is zero. The restriction map in (1.1) is therefore surjective, including for the entire possibly nonreduced divisor scheme \\(E\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Corollary 2.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((H,\\Theta)\\) be a normal connected projective complex klt pair with effective rational boundary and nef \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier adjoint \\(D_H=K_H+\\Theta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\pi:V\\to H\\) be a projective log resolution, and put \\(N=\\pi^*D_H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\varphi\\) be a minimal semipositive weight on the actual rational line bundle \\(N\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(t&gt;0\\), let \\(\\mathcal I(t\\varphi)\\) consist of holomorphic germs \\(f\\) for which \\(|f|^2e^{-t\\varphi}\\) is locally integrable.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every real \\(t&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(t\\varphi)=\\mathcal O_V.\n\\]\nThe conclusion is independent of the choice of minimal metric and of the Cartier multiple used to normalize its weights.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Theorem 1, \\(\\varphi\\) has zero Lelong numbers everywhere. Skoda’s integrability theorem gives local integrability of \\(e^{-t\\varphi}\\) for every fixed \\(t&gt;0\\) [21, Proposition 7.1]; for arbitrary plurisubharmonic weights, see [5, Property 1.4(8)] and [4, Lemma 5.6(a)].</p><p>Every holomorphic germ is bounded on a smaller neighborhood, so\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(t\\varphi)=\\mathcal O_V.\n\\]</p><p>Two metrics with minimal singularities have weights differing by a bounded function, directly from their defining comparison. Dividing the weight of a positive Cartier multiple by that multiple preserves the stated normalization. These observations prove both independence assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "Corollary 4.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((X,\\Delta)\\) be a projective complex klt fourfold with effective rational boundary.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that the rational adjoint \\(D=K_X+\\Delta\\) is nef.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that \\(\\chi(X,\\mathcal O_X)\\neq0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The divisor \\(D\\) is semiample. No restriction on the numerical dimension of \\(D\\) is imposed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a projective log resolution \\(\\pi:Y\\to X\\), a positive integer \\(r\\) such that \\(rD\\) is Cartier, and a metric \\(h_{\\min}\\) with minimal singularities on the actual rational line bundle\n\\[\nL=\\pi^*D.\n\\]\nNefness makes \\(D\\) pseudoeffective, so such a metric exists. By Theorem 1,\n\\[\n\\nu(h_{\\min},y)=0\\qquad(y\\in Y).\n\\]</p><p>The tensor power \\(h_{\\min}^{\\otimes r}\\) is a metric on\n\\[\n\\pi^*\\mathcal O_X(rD).\n\\]\nIts local weights are \\(r\\varphi\\), so its curvature is semipositive and its Lelong numbers are\n\\[\nr\\nu(\\varphi,y)=0.\n\\]\nConsequently, the metric condition (4.1) in Proposition 9 holds with \\(T_0\\) equal to this curvature current and with zero divisor sum.</p><p>Apply Proposition 9 with \\(N=0\\) and \\(t=0\\). Since \\(\\chi(X,\\mathcal O_X)\\neq0\\), this gives\n\\[\n\\kappa(X,D)\\geq0.\n\\]\nThus a positive Cartier multiple of \\(D\\) has a nonzero global section. This is exactly the Kodaira-dimension hypothesis of Proposition 10. The same resolution and the same metric \\(h_{\\min}^{\\otimes r}\\) supply its remaining metric hypothesis. Proposition 10 proves that \\(D\\) is semiample.</p><p>The Euler-characteristic hypothesis is used only to obtain the first section. No numerical-dimension restriction is imposed, and Theorem 2 is not an input to this application.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((M,\\omega_M)\\) be a complete Kähler manifold of dimension \\(n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F\\) be a holomorphic line bundle with a smooth Hermitian metric of local weight \\(\\psi\\) and strictly positive curvature \\(\\theta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\Lambda_{\\omega_M}\\) for the adjoint of wedging with \\(\\omega_M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\beta\\) be an \\(F\\)-valued, \\(\\bar\\partial\\)-closed \\((n,1)\\)-form which is square integrable and satisfies\n  \\[\n  I(\\beta)=\n  \\int_M\n  \\left\\langle[\\theta,\\Lambda_{\\omega_M}]^{-1}\\beta,\\beta\n  \\right\\rangle_{\\psi,\\omega_M}\\,dV_{\\omega_M}&lt;\\infty.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For an \\(F\\)-valued top form \\(v\\), the expression \\(|v|^2e^{-\\psi}\\) denotes the coordinate-invariant top-form density.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an \\(F\\)-valued \\((n,0)\\)-form \\(v\\) with\n\\[\n\\bar\\partial v=\\beta,\n\\qquad\n\\int_M|v|^2e^{-\\psi}\\leq I(\\beta).\n\\]</p><p>In local coordinates, the inverse-curvature density in \\(I(\\beta)\\) is the inverse Hermitian curvature matrix acting on the remaining \\((0,1)\\)-covector, multiplied by the coordinate top-form density. It is independent of the complete background metric. Consequently:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(\\theta\\geq\\omega_0&gt;0\\), this density is bounded by the \\((n,1)\\)-norm density computed with \\(\\omega_0\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(g\\) is a smooth real function and \\(\\theta\\geq a\\,i\\partial g\\wedge\\bar\\partial g\\), where \\(a&gt;0\\), then the inverse-curvature squared norm of \\(\\bar\\partial g\\) is at most \\(a^{-1}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The existence statement is the complete Kähler \\(L^2\\) theorem with positive curvature operator and finite inverse-curvature integral [4, Theorem 5.1, p. 33]. It requires pointwise positivity and the displayed integral, not a uniform lower spectral bound relative to \\(\\omega_M\\).</p><p>For the density calculation, write a top-form-valued \\((0,1)\\)-form as\n\\[\n\\sum_j\\beta_j\\,dz_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge dz_n\\wedge d\\bar z_j.\n\\]\nThe determinant in the squared norm of the top form cancels the volume determinant. Diagonalizing the curvature relative to \\(\\omega_M\\) shows that applying the inverse commutator leaves\n\\[\n\\sum_{j,k}(\\theta^{-1})^{j\\bar k}\\beta_j\\overline{\\beta_k}\n\\]\nagainst the coordinate volume and line weight. All formulas use the same standard normalization of forms and volume.</p><p>Matrix order gives \\(\\theta^{-1}\\leq\\omega_0^{-1}\\) in the first consequence. For the second, conjugate the rank-one inequality\n\\[\na\\,\\partial g\\otimes\\overline{\\partial g}\\leq\\theta\n\\]\nby \\(\\theta^{-1/2}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fixed twist, normalized extremal sections, and vanishing sublevel mass.", "statement": "<p>Fixed twist, normalized extremal sections, and vanishing sublevel mass.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((H,\\Theta)\\) be a normal connected projective complex klt pair with effective rational boundary and nef \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier adjoint \\(D_H=K_H+\\Theta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\pi:V\\to H\\) be a projective log resolution with \\(n=\\dim V&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose compatible canonical divisors and write\n  \\[\n  N=\\pi^*D_H=K_V+B.\n  \\tag{2.1}\n  \\]\n  The support of \\(B\\) is simple normal crossing, every coefficient is strictly less than one, and the negative part \\(B^-\\) is exceptional over \\(H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b=[B]\\) be the divisor weight. In crossing coordinates,\n  \\[\n  b=\\sum_{j=1}^{\\ell}\\beta_j\\log|z_j|^2+O(1),\n  \\qquad \\beta_j&lt;1.\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(e^{-b}\\) is locally integrable.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a very ample Cartier divisor \\(H_0\\) on \\(H\\), put\n  \\[\n  A_H=(n+1)H_0,\\qquad A=\\pi^*A_H,\n  \\]\n  and give \\(A\\) a smooth semipositive metric with weight \\(a\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal M=\\{m\\in\\mathbb Z_{&gt;0}:mD_H\\text{ is Cartier}\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a semipositive metric \\(\\varphi\\) with minimal singularities on \\(N\\), so that every other semipositive weight \\(\\psi\\) on \\(N\\) satisfies\n  \\[\n  \\psi\\leq\\varphi+C_\\psi.\n  \\tag{2.2}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(p\\in V\\). For \\(m\\in\\mathcal M\\), define\n  \\[\n  Q_m(s)=\\int_V|s|^{2/m}e^{-a/m-b},\n  \\qquad s\\in H^0(V,mN+A).\n  \\tag{2.3}\n  \\]\n  By \\(N=K_V+B\\), the integrand is a canonical density.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(m\\in\\mathcal M\\), the bundle \\(mN+A\\) is globally generated, and there is a section \\(s_m\\) maximizing absolute evaluation at \\(p\\) among sections with \\(Q_m=1\\). It satisfies\n\\[\nQ_m(s_m)=1,\\qquad s_m(p)\\neq0.\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]</p><p>Put \\(\\tau_m=m^{-1}\\log|s_m|^2\\), a weight on \\(N+A/m\\). After passage to an unbounded subsequence in \\(\\mathcal M\\), the weights \\(\\tau_m-a/m\\) are uniformly locally bounded above and converge locally in \\(L^1\\) and almost everywhere to a semipositive weight \\(\\tau_\\infty\\) on \\(N\\).</p><p>Choose \\(C_\\infty\\) with \\(\\tau_\\infty\\leq\\varphi+C_\\infty\\), and fix \\(R&gt;C_\\infty+1\\). For the globally scalar difference\n\\[\ng_m=\\varphi+a/m-\\tau_m\n\\]\ndefined almost everywhere, one has\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_m:=\n\\int_{\\{g_m&lt;-R\\}}e^{\\tau_m-a/m-b}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\nValues on the zero divisor of \\(s_m\\) may be assigned arbitrarily when taking integrals.</p>", "proof": "<p>The nonexceptional coefficients of \\(B\\) are those of the effective boundary \\(\\Theta\\), so \\(B^-\\) is exceptional over \\(H\\). The klt condition gives \\(\\beta_j&lt;1\\), hence local integrability of \\(e^{-b}\\).</p><p>Since \\(N\\) is nef, it is pseudo-effective. A semipositive metric with minimal singularities is obtained as the upper envelope, relative to a smooth reference weight, of normalized plurisubharmonic weights [2, Definition 1.4 and Theorem 1.5].</p><p>Lemma 2.1 gives the extremal sections and their normalization. Lemma 2.2 gives the subsequence and its semipositive limit. Minimality yields\n\\[\n\\tau_\\infty\\leq\\varphi+C_\\infty.\n\\]\nChoose \\(R&gt;C_\\infty+1\\). Where both weights are finite,\n\\[\n\\varphi-\\tau_\\infty\\geq-C_\\infty,\n\\]\nso the indicators of \\(\\{g_m&lt;-R\\}\\) tend to zero almost everywhere. The densities \\(e^{\\tau_m-a/m-b}\\) have one integrable majorant on a fixed finite coordinate cover, by the local upper bounds and integrability of \\(e^{-b}\\). Dominated convergence proves (2.5).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Fixed global generation and the extremal normalization.", "statement": "<p>Fixed global generation and the extremal normalization.</p><p>In the setting of Proposition 2, for every \\(m\\in\\mathcal M\\):</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(mD_H+A_H\\), and hence \\(mN+A\\), is globally generated.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. \\(Q_m\\) is finite and continuous, positive away from zero, and homogeneous of degree \\(2/m\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The set \\(\\{s:Q_m(s)=1\\}\\) is compact, and an absolute-evaluation maximizer \\(s_m\\) at \\(p\\) satisfies \\(s_m(p)\\neq0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(1\\leq j\\leq n\\),\n\\[\nmD_H+A_H-jH_0-(K_H+\\Theta)\n=(m-1)D_H+(n+1-j)H_0\n\\]\nis ample. Klt Kawamata–Viehweg vanishing, in the form of [9, Theorem 3.2], gives the cohomology vanishings for Castelnuovo–Mumford regularity. The global-generation conclusion of [15, Theorem 1.8.5(i)] then applies. Pullback proves that \\(mN+A\\) is globally generated as well.</p><p>The klt coefficients imply local integrability of \\(e^{-b}\\), including where \\(B\\) has negative exceptional coefficients. The integral defining \\(Q_m\\) is therefore finite and continuous in \\(s\\), positive for \\(s\\neq0\\), and homogeneous of degree \\(2/m\\).</p><p>Restrict \\(Q_m\\) first to the unit sphere of any vector-space norm on the finite-dimensional section space and use its positive minimum. These facts show that \\(Q_m(s)=1\\) is compact. Choose \\(s_m\\) maximizing absolute evaluation at \\(p\\), using any fixed norm on the one-dimensional fiber for this \\(m\\). Global generation implies \\(s_m(p)\\neq0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Claim 2.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the bundles, weights, and gauge \\(Q_m\\) of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(m\\) tend to infinity through allowed integers in \\(\\mathcal M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(s_m\\in H^0(V,mN+A)\\) be any sequence with \\(Q_m(s_m)=1\\), and put \\(\\tau_m=m^{-1}\\log|s_m|^2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After passage to an unbounded subsequence, the weights \\(\\tau_m-a/m\\) are uniformly locally bounded above and converge locally in \\(L^1\\) and almost everywhere to a semipositive weight \\(\\tau_\\infty\\) on \\(N\\). This assertion does not require extremality at \\(p\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a smooth reference weight \\(\\varphi_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\) on \\(N\\) and write\n\\[\nu_m=\\tau_m-a/m-\\varphi_{\\mathrm{ref}}.\n\\]\nThese are global quasi-psh functions whose complex Hessians have one common lower bound \\(-C\\omega\\) on the compact connected manifold \\(V\\), for a fixed Kähler form \\(\\omega\\). Normalize them by subtracting\n\\[\nM_m=\\sup_Vu_m.\n\\]\nThe compactness theorem for sup-normalized quasi-psh functions [12, Proposition 2.7] gives, along a subsequence, local \\(L^1\\) and almost-everywhere convergence of \\(u_m-M_m\\) to a quasi-psh function \\(u_\\infty\\) that is finite almost everywhere. In particular, the normalized functions do not converge identically to \\(-\\infty\\).</p><p>The fixed density \\(e^{\\varphi_{\\mathrm{ref}}-b}\\) is integrable. Since \\(u_m-M_m\\leq0\\), dominated convergence gives\n\\[\nJ_m:=\n\\int_Ve^{u_m-M_m}e^{\\varphi_{\\mathrm{ref}}-b}\n\\longrightarrow\nJ_\\infty:=\n\\int_Ve^{u_\\infty}e^{\\varphi_{\\mathrm{ref}}-b}\n\\in(0,\\infty).\n\\]\nThe positivity uses finiteness of \\(u_\\infty\\) almost everywhere, not a pointwise lower bound. The normalization says\n\\[\ne^{M_m}J_m=1.\n\\]\nThus \\(M_m\\) converges to a finite real number, proving the asserted upper bounds and convergence without the sup normalization.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\ni\\partial\\bar\\partial(\\tau_m-a/m)\n\\geq-m^{-1}i\\partial\\bar\\partial a;\n\\]\nthe error tends to zero. The limit weight is therefore semipositive.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemma 2.3, “Localization of top forms.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U\\) be a smooth connected affine complex variety of dimension \\(n\\geq1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F\\) be a holomorphic line bundle on \\(U\\) with a smooth semipositive metric of weight \\(S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g\\) be a global plurisubharmonic function not identically \\(-\\infty\\), and let \\(s\\) be a holomorphic \\(F\\)-valued \\((n,0)\\)-form.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(R\\in\\mathbb R\\), and a smooth cutoff \\(0\\leq\\chi\\leq1\\) equal to one for \\(t\\leq-R-1\\) and zero for \\(t\\geq-R\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f\\) be a smooth convex function which is affine of positive slope \\(c\\) near \\(-\\infty\\), satisfies \\(c\\leq f'\\leq d\\) for some \\(d\\), and has \\(f''&gt;0\\) on \\([-R-1,-R]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n  \\[\n  M=\\int_{\\{g&lt;-R\\}}|s|^2e^{-S}&lt;\\infty.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a measurable \\(F\\)-valued top form \\(u\\) such that, distributionally,\n\\[\n\\bar\\partial u=\\bar\\partial(\\chi(g)s),\n\\qquad\n\\int_U|u|^2e^{-S-f(g)}\\leq C_{\\chi,f}M,\n\\tag{2.9}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nC_{\\chi,f}\n=\\max_{[-R-1,-R]}\\frac{|\\chi'|^2e^{-f}}{f''}.\n\\tag{2.10}\n\\]\nThe constant is independent of \\(U,F,S,g,s\\) and of the auxiliary complete Kähler metrics. At \\(g=-\\infty\\), set \\(\\chi(g)=1\\) and \\(f(g)=-\\infty\\); that locus has measure zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>Embed \\(U\\) as a closed complex submanifold of some \\(\\mathbb C^a\\) and let \\(\\rho=|z|^2|_U\\). The tubular-neighborhood theorem supplies a holomorphic retraction of a neighborhood onto \\(U\\) [6]; see also [8, Theorem 3.1]. Compose \\(g\\) with the retraction. Radial convolution in that neighborhood, at radii decreasing to zero, gives smooth psh functions \\(g_j\\downarrow g\\) on common smaller domains.</p><p>More precisely, choose increasing regular exhaustion values \\(a_j\\to\\infty\\) and\n\\[\n\\Omega_j=\\{\\rho&lt;a_j\\}.\n\\]\nUse one fixed nonnegative smooth radial averaging kernel of total mass one. The convolution radius at stage \\(j\\) is at most \\(1/j\\), is small enough to work near \\(\\overline{\\Omega_j}\\), and is smaller than all previous radii. Radial averages of a psh function are monotone in the radius, so these choices give monotonicity wherever two functions are defined. They also give \\(g_j\\geq g\\).</p><p>Each \\(\\Omega_j\\) has a complete Kähler metric dominating \\(i\\partial\\bar\\partial\\rho\\), for example the metric with potential\n\\[\n\\rho-\\log(a_j-\\rho).\n\\]\nFor a decreasing positive sequence \\(\\delta_j\\to0\\), use the line weight\n\\[\nP_j=S+f(g_j)+\\delta_j\\rho.\n\\]\nIt is smooth near \\(\\overline{\\Omega_j}\\), its curvature is strictly positive, and\n\\[\ni\\partial\\bar\\partial P_j\n\\geq f''(g_j)i\\partial g_j\\wedge\\bar\\partial g_j.\n\\]</p><p>The smooth closed datum\n\\[\n\\beta_j=\\chi'(g_j)\\bar\\partial g_j\\wedge s\n\\]\nis square integrable for the complete metric. Even near the boundary, enlarging the background metric decreases the \\((n,1)\\)-density, and all coefficients and line weights are smooth on a neighborhood of the relatively compact closure.</p><p>By Proposition 1, the inverse-curvature integral is at most\n\\[\nC\\int_{\\Omega_j\\cap\\{g_j&lt;-R\\}}|s|^2e^{-S}\n\\leq C\\int_{\\{g&lt;-R\\}}|s|^2e^{-S}=CM,\n\\qquad\nC=\\sup_{[-R-1,-R]}\\frac{|\\chi'|^2e^{-f}}{f''}.\n\\]\nThe nonnegative term \\(\\delta_j\\rho\\) only decreases the integrand. Thus there are solutions \\(u_j\\) on \\(\\Omega_j\\) with\n\\[\n\\bar\\partial u_j=\\bar\\partial(\\chi(g_j)s),\n\\qquad\n\\int_{\\Omega_j}|u_j|^2e^{-P_j}\\leq CM.\n\\]\nThe background complete metrics have introduced no comparison factor.</p><p>For the singular passage, use fixed Hilbert spaces. If \\(k\\leq l\\) are fixed and \\(j\\geq l\\), then on \\(\\Omega_k\\), \\(P_j\\leq P_l\\), and hence\n\\[\n\\int_{\\Omega_k}|u_j|^2e^{-P_l}\\leq CM.\n\\]\nTake a diagonal weakly convergent subsequence in these countably many fixed earlier smooth weighted spaces. Their limits agree as distributions on overlaps and define \\(u\\) on \\(U\\). Weak lower semicontinuity, then monotone convergence as \\(l\\to\\infty\\), gives\n\\[\n\\int_{\\Omega_k}|u|^2e^{-S-f(g)}\\leq CM.\n\\]\nExhausting \\(U\\) proves the global inequality. Finally,\n\\[\n\\chi(g_j)s\\longrightarrow\\chi(g)s\n\\]\nlocally in \\(L^2\\) by bounded convergence. Thus the equations pass to distributions and give (2.9).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Global competitors at a proposed positive-Lelong point.", "statement": "<p>Global competitors at a proposed positive-Lelong point.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((H,\\Theta)\\), \\(\\pi:V\\to H\\), \\(N=K_V+B\\), \\(A=\\pi^*A_H\\), and the weights \\(a,b,\\varphi\\) be as in Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(n=\\dim V&gt;0\\) and that \\(\\varphi\\) has positive Lelong number at a point \\(p\\in V\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Along the subsequence supplied by Proposition 2, let \\(s_m\\), \\(\\tau_m=m^{-1}\\log|s_m|^2\\), \\(g_m=\\varphi+a/m-\\tau_m\\), \\(R\\), and \\(\\varepsilon_m\\) have the meanings stated there. In particular,\n  \\[\n  Q_m(s_m)=1,\\qquad s_m(p)\\neq0,\\qquad \\varepsilon_m\\to0.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a constant \\(C_0\\), independent of \\(m\\), such that for all sufficiently large integers in this subsequence there are global sections\n\\[\nw_m\\in H^0(V,mN+A)\n\\]\nsatisfying\n\\[\nw_m(p)=s_m(p),\n\\qquad\nQ_m(w_m)^m\\leq C_0\\varepsilon_m.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 4.1 fixes the singularity and growth requirements for the localization weight. Lemma 4.2 constructs the global section and preserves evaluation at \\(p\\). Lemma 4.3 gives\n\\[\nQ_m(w_m)^m\\leq C'\\varepsilon_m(1+I)^d.\n\\]\nTaking \\(C_0=C'(1+I)^d\\), independent of \\(m\\), proves the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A nonintegrable weight and a fixed convex profile.", "statement": "<p>A nonintegrable weight and a fixed convex profile.</p><p>In the setting of Proposition 4, there are fixed constants \\(c&gt;0\\), \\(d\\geq c\\), \\(C_f\\), a smooth cutoff \\(0\\leq\\chi\\leq1\\), and a smooth convex increasing function \\(f\\), all independent of \\(m\\), such that:\n\\[\ne^{-c\\varphi-b}\\quad\\text{is not locally integrable at }p;\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\\(\\chi=1\\) on \\((-\\infty,-R-1]\\) and \\(\\chi=0\\) on \\([-R,\\infty)\\); \\(f\\) is affine of slope \\(c\\) near \\(-\\infty\\); \\(f''&gt;0\\) on \\([-R-1,-R]\\); \\(c\\leq f'\\leq d\\); and\n\\[\nf(t)\\leq d\\max(0,t)+C_f\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Positive Lelong number gives local coordinates at \\(p\\) and a sufficiently small fixed \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\) with\n\\[\n\\varphi(z)\\leq\\alpha\\log|z|^2+O(1).\n\\]\nWrite the crossing divisor weight near \\(p\\) as\n\\[\nb=\\sum_{j=1}^{\\ell}\\beta_j\\log|z_j|^2+O(1).\n\\]\nOn a cone of positive angular measure on which all crossing coordinates have modulus comparable to \\(|z|\\), the weight \\(e^{-c\\varphi-b}\\) is bounded below by a positive constant times\n\\[\n|z|^{-2(c\\alpha+\\sum_j\\beta_j)}.\n\\]\nChoose a fixed \\(c&gt;0\\) large enough that\n\\[\nc\\alpha+\\sum_j\\beta_j\\geq n.\n\\]\nThis proves (2.6), even if some \\(\\beta_j\\) are negative.</p><p>Choose \\(\\chi\\) as stated. Choose \\(f\\) by prescribing a nonnegative compactly supported second derivative positive on the transition interval. Then \\(f\\) is affine of slope \\(c\\) near \\(-\\infty\\), and \\(c\\leq f'\\leq d\\) for one fixed \\(d\\). In particular, (2.7) holds for one fixed \\(C_f\\). All these choices remain fixed as \\(m\\) varies.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Localization, extension, and preservation of evaluation.", "statement": "<p>Localization, extension, and preservation of evaluation.</p><p>In the setting of Proposition 4, choose \\(\\chi,f,c,d,C_f\\) as in Lemma 4.1. For each fixed \\(m&gt;d+1\\), choose a dense smooth affine open\n\\[\nU=U_m\\subset V\n\\]\navoiding the supports of \\(B\\) and \\(\\operatorname{div}(s_m)\\). Put\n\\[\nF_m=mN+A-K_V,\\qquad\nS=(m-1)\\tau_m+a/m+b,\n\\tag{2.8}\n\\]\nand, on \\(U\\), put \\(g=g_m\\) and\n\\[\nG=S+d\\max(0,g).\n\\]</p><p>There is a holomorphic section \\(w_m\\) on \\(U\\) which extends to a global section of \\(mN+A\\), satisfies\n\\[\n\\int_U|w_m|^2e^{-G}\\leq C'\\varepsilon_m\n\\tag{2.13}\n\\]\nfor a constant \\(C'\\) independent of \\(m\\), and has\n\\[\nw_m(p)=s_m(p)\\neq0.\n\\tag{2.15}\n\\]\nThe affine open need not contain \\(p\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>On \\(U\\), \\(g\\) is psh: \\(\\tau_m\\) is locally pluriharmonic there, and \\(\\varphi,a\\) are semipositive. The weight \\(S\\) is smooth and semipositive on \\(F_m|_U\\). Divisor weights and \\(\\tau_m\\) are pluriharmonic on \\(U\\), so its curvature is\n\\[\nm^{-1}i\\partial\\bar\\partial a.\n\\]\nRegard \\(s_m\\) as an \\(F_m\\)-valued top form. The exact density identity is\n\\[\n|s_m|^2e^{-S}=e^{\\tau_m-a/m-b}.\n\\tag{2.11}\n\\]\nIts sublevel mass is \\(\\varepsilon_m\\). Proposition 3 gives an \\(F_m\\)-valued top form \\(u\\) on \\(U\\) with\n\\[\n\\bar\\partial u=\\bar\\partial(\\chi(g)s_m),\n\\qquad\n\\int_U|u|^2e^{-S-f(g)}\\leq C\\varepsilon_m,\n\\tag{2.12}\n\\]\nwhere \\(C=C_{\\chi,f}\\) is independent of \\(m\\) and \\(U\\).</p><p>Set\n\\[\nw_m=\\chi(g)s_m-u.\n\\]\nIt is holomorphic. Since \\(G\\geq S\\), the cutoff term has \\(G\\)-norm squared at most \\(\\varepsilon_m\\). Since \\(S+f(g)\\leq G+C_f\\), the \\(G\\)-norm squared of \\(u\\) is at most \\(e^{C_f}C\\varepsilon_m\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\int_U|w_m|^2e^{-G}\\leq C'\\varepsilon_m,\n\\qquad C'=2+2e^{C_f}C.\n\\]</p><p>To extend the section, rewrite the weight as\n\\[\nG=b+a/m+(m-1-d)\\tau_m\n+d\\max\\{\\tau_m,\\varphi+a/m\\}.\n\\tag{2.14}\n\\]\nIt is locally bounded above away from \\(\\operatorname{Supp}B^-\\): the coefficient \\(m-1-d\\) is positive, the psh weights in the last two terms are locally bounded above, and \\(b\\) has that property away from its negative components. Thus (2.13) gives ordinary local \\(L^2\\) bounds there. Holomorphic \\(L^2\\) removal across analytic sets extends \\(w_m\\) to\n\\[\nV\\setminus\\operatorname{Supp}B^-.\n\\]</p><p>There is an open \\(H^\\circ\\subset H\\), with complement of codimension at least two, over which \\(\\pi\\) is an isomorphism. It misses the image of \\(B^-\\). The section descends on \\(H^\\circ\\) to a section of the actual Cartier bundle\n\\[\n\\mathcal O_H(mD_H+A_H).\n\\]\nNormality extends it across \\(H\\setminus H^\\circ\\): after trivializing that line bundle, this is Hartogs extension of regular holomorphic functions on a normal space. Pullback gives a global section of \\(mN+A\\). By projectivity and GAGA it is algebraic as well. It agrees with the constructed section on \\(U\\) by the identity theorem. No estimate across the negative exceptional divisor is needed.</p><p>Near \\(p\\), \\(\\tau_m\\) is smooth because \\(s_m(p)\\neq0\\), whereas \\(g\\to-\\infty\\). Thus \\(\\chi(g)=1\\) and \\(f(g)=cg+\\text{constant}\\) near \\(p\\) on \\(U\\), so\n\\[\nu=s_m-w_m,\n\\qquad\nS+f(g)=b+c\\varphi+O_m(1).\n\\]\nIf \\(s_m(p)-w_m(p)\\) were nonzero, its holomorphic coefficient would have a positive lower bound on a smaller neighborhood. The finite weighted integral in (2.12) would contradict (2.6). Removing the analytic null set \\(V\\setminus U\\) does not change this divergence. This also applies when \\(p\\) lies on \\(B^-\\), since both sections are now holomorphic at \\(p\\).</p><p>Hence \\(w_m(p)=s_m(p)\\neq0\\). The bounded factor \\(O_m(1)\\) is used only to test integrability for a fixed \\(m\\); it never enters (2.13).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Uniform conversion from the quadratic estimate to the gauge.", "statement": "<p>Uniform conversion from the quadratic estimate to the gauge.</p><p>In the setting of Lemma 4.2, define\n\\[\nI=\\int_Ve^{\\varphi-b}.\n\\]\nThen \\(I&lt;\\infty\\), and for \\(m&gt;d+1\\),\n\\[\nQ_m(w_m)^m\\leq C'\\varepsilon_m(1+I)^d.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Hölder’s inequality gives\n\\[\nQ_m(w_m)^m\n\\leq C'\\varepsilon_m\n\\left(\\int_Ue^{(G-a-mb)/(m-1)}\\right)^{m-1}.\n\\tag{2.16}\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\n\\eta=\\frac d{m-1}\\in(0,1).\n\\]\nDirect expansion yields\n\\[\n\\frac{G-a-mb}{m-1}\n=(1-\\eta)(\\tau_m-a/m-b)\n+\\eta\\bigl(\\max\\{\\tau_m-a/m,\\varphi\\}-b\\bigr).\n\\tag{2.17}\n\\]\nBoth exponentials on the right are canonical densities. The first has integral one; the second has integral at most \\(1+I\\).</p><p>Finiteness of \\(I\\) follows from local upper bounds for \\(\\varphi\\) and local integrability of \\(e^{-b}\\) on a fixed finite coordinate cover. Applying Hölder to (2.17) gives\n\\[\n\\left(\\int_Ue^{(G-a-mb)/(m-1)}\\right)^{m-1}\n\\leq(1+I)^{\\eta(m-1)}=(1+I)^d.\n\\]\nAll integrals are unchanged by the omitted analytic null set. Combining this with (2.16) gives the asserted estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemma 3.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F\\) be a rational line bundle on a smooth projective complex variety \\(V\\), equipped with a Kähler form \\(\\omega\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(F\\) has a semipositive singular metric \\(\\psi\\) having zero Lelong numbers everywhere.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are smooth weights \\(\\psi_k\\) on \\(F\\) and numbers \\(\\epsilon_k\\downarrow0\\) such that\n\\[\ni\\partial\\bar\\partial\\psi_k\\geq-\\epsilon_k\\omega.\n\\]\nIn fixed local frames, these weights are uniformly bounded above on relatively compact coordinate neighborhoods, and, for every fixed \\(b&gt;0\\), the functions \\(e^{-b\\psi_k}\\) have uniformly bounded local \\(L^1\\) norms.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a smooth reference metric on \\(F\\). Apply the attenuation form of Demailly’s regularization theorem to\n\\[\nT=i\\partial\\bar\\partial\\psi,\n\\]\nusing the global difference between \\(\\psi\\) and the reference weight as its potential [3, Main Theorem 1.1]. Fix a positive attenuation level \\(c\\). Adding the reference weight back to the approximating potentials gives weights \\(\\psi_k\\) on the same rational bundle, with \\(\\psi_k\\downarrow\\psi\\). They are smooth outside the Lelong level set\n\\[\nE_c(T)=\\{x:\\nu(T,x)\\geq c\\},\n\\]\nwhich is empty.</p><p>Their curvature lower bounds have the form\n\\[\n-\\min(\\lambda_k,c)u-\\delta_k\\omega,\n\\]\nwhere \\(u\\) is one fixed smooth nonnegative form, \\(\\delta_k\\to0\\), and the continuous functions \\(\\lambda_k\\) decrease to the Lelong-number function. That function is zero here. Dini’s theorem on the compact manifold gives \\(\\sup_V\\lambda_k\\to0\\), and \\(u\\leq C\\omega\\) gives the stated loss \\(\\epsilon_k\\to0\\). Passing to a subsequence makes the losses decrease. Work first on a common integral tensor power of \\(F\\) and divide all weights by that integer afterwards.</p><p>On each fixed coordinate neighborhood,\n\\[\n\\psi\\leq\\psi_k\\leq\\psi_1.\n\\]\nThe upper bound is smooth on a slightly larger neighborhood, and hence bounded on the chosen compact subset. For every fixed \\(b&gt;0\\),\n\\[\ne^{-b\\psi_k}\\leq e^{-b\\psi}.\n\\]\nThe right side is locally integrable by the zero-Lelong case of Skoda’s integrability criterion [21]; see [5, Property 1.4(8)] for arbitrary plurisubharmonic weights. This proves the uniform fixed-exponent bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Endpoint comparison weights and a complete metric.", "statement": "<p>Endpoint comparison weights and a complete metric.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V,L,C_0,C_2,\\lambda,C_1,L_0,L_1\\) satisfy the assumptions and definitions of Theorem 2, and assume \\(n=\\dim V&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a Kähler form \\(\\omega\\) on \\(V\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Apply Proposition 5 to the specified endpoint metrics on \\(L-C_0\\) and \\(L-C_2\\), obtaining smooth weights \\(\\psi_{0,k},\\psi_{2,k}\\) with a common sequence \\(0&lt;\\epsilon_k\\leq1\\) tending to zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  D=\\operatorname{Supp}(C_0+C_2)_{\\mathrm{red}},\n  \\qquad U=V\\setminus D.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a rational divisor \\(C\\), write \\([C]\\) for its divisor weight.</p><p class=\"li\">On \\(U\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\psi'_{0,k}\n  =\\log\\!\\left(e^{\\psi_{0,k}}+\n  e^{\\psi_{2,k}+[C_2-C_0]}\\right),\n  \\tag{3.1}\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\Phi_{0,k}=\\psi'_{0,k}+[C_0],\n  \\qquad\n  \\Phi_{2,k}=\\psi_{2,k}+[C_2],\n  \\qquad\n  \\Phi_{1,k}=(1-\\lambda)\\Phi_{0,k}+\\lambda\\Phi_{2,k},\n  \\tag{3.2}\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  h_{i,k}=\\Phi_{i,k}-[\\lfloor C_i\\rfloor],\n  \\qquad i=0,1.\n  \\tag{3.3}\n  \\]\n  The \\(\\Phi_{i,k}\\) are weights on \\(L\\), and \\(h_{i,k}\\) are weights on \\(L_i|_U\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E=\\lfloor C_1\\rfloor-\\lfloor C_0\\rfloor\\geq0\\), and let \\(s:L_1\\to L_0\\) be multiplication by the canonical section of \\(E\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The weights \\(h_{i,k}\\) are smooth on \\(U\\), uniformly locally bounded above in frames extending across \\(D\\), and satisfy\n\\[\n\\sup_k\\int_{B\\cap U}e^{-h_{i,k}}\\,d\\lambda_B&lt;\\infty\n\\qquad(i=0,1)\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\non every relatively compact coordinate neighborhood \\(B\\), where \\(d\\lambda_B\\) is coordinate volume.</p><p>There is a complete Kähler metric \\(\\omega_c\\geq\\omega\\) on \\(U\\), with bounded local potentials on sufficiently small coordinate neighborhoods in \\(V\\), and compactly supported smooth cutoffs tending to one whose gradients tend uniformly to zero in \\(\\omega_c\\).</p><p>Multiplication by \\(s\\) is a contraction in every form degree:\n\\[\n|s|^2e^{h_{1,k}-h_{0,k}}\n=e^{\\Phi_{1,k}-\\Phi_{0,k}}\\leq1.\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nWriting\n\\[\n\\theta_{i,k}=i\\partial\\bar\\partial\\Phi_{i,k}\\quad\\text{on }U,\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\theta_{1,k}+\\epsilon_k\\omega_c\n\\geq(1-\\lambda)(\\theta_{0,k}+\\epsilon_k\\omega_c)\\geq0.\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 6.1 gives the weight bounds, integrability, contraction, and curvature identities. Lemma 6.2 gives the complete metric with bounded local potentials and the cutoffs.</p><p>Since \\(\\omega_c\\geq\\omega\\),\n\\[\n\\theta_{0,k}+\\epsilon_k\\omega_c\\geq0,\n\\qquad\n\\theta_{2,k}+\\epsilon_k\\omega_c\\geq0.\n\\]\nUsing\n\\[\n\\theta_{1,k}+\\epsilon_k\\omega_c\n=(1-\\lambda)(\\theta_{0,k}+\\epsilon_k\\omega_c)\n+\\lambda(\\theta_{2,k}+\\epsilon_k\\omega_c)\n\\]\nproves (3.6).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Properties of the endpoint comparison weights.", "statement": "<p>Properties of the endpoint comparison weights.</p><p>Under the endpoint assumptions and definitions of Proposition 6:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The logarithmic sum defining \\(\\psi'_{0,k}\\) is intrinsically well-defined on \\(L-C_0\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. It retains the uniform local upper bounds and all fixed-exponent integrability bounds supplied by Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The weights \\(h_{0,k},h_{1,k}\\) have the upper bounds and uniform integrals in (3.4).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. On \\(U\\),\n   \\[\n   \\Phi_{2,k}\\leq\\Phi_{0,k},\\qquad\n   \\Phi_{1,k}\\leq\\Phi_{0,k},\n   \\]\n   and (3.5) holds.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. The endpoint curvatures satisfy \\(\\theta_{0,k},\\theta_{2,k}\\geq-\\epsilon_k\\omega\\), and\n   \\[\n   \\theta_{1,k}=(1-\\lambda)\\theta_{0,k}+\\lambda\\theta_{2,k}.\n   \\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The two summands in (3.1) are exponentials of weights on the same rational line bundle \\(L-C_0\\), so the expression is intrinsically well-defined. The logarithm of a sum of exponentials preserves the common curvature lower bound: locally add a potential for \\(\\epsilon_k\\omega\\) to both weights and use the psh log-sum inequality.</p><p>Moreover, \\(\\psi'_{0,k}\\geq\\psi_{0,k}\\). Effectivity of \\(C_2-C_0\\) gives a uniform local upper bound for its divisor weight, so \\(\\psi'_{0,k}\\) retains both the upper bounds and all fixed-exponent integrability bounds.</p><p>The weights \\(h_{i,k}\\) are smooth on \\(U\\) and uniformly locally bounded above in frames extending across \\(D\\). For integrability, write\n\\[\nh_{0,k}=\\psi'_{0,k}+[C_0-\\lfloor C_0\\rfloor],\n\\]\n\\[\nh_{1,k}\n=(1-\\lambda)\\psi'_{0,k}+\\lambda\\psi_{2,k}\n+[C_1-\\lfloor C_1\\rfloor].\n\\]\nEvery coefficient of either fractional divisor lies in \\([0,1)\\). Choose one Hölder exponent \\(a&gt;1\\) so close to one that its product with each of these finitely many coefficients is still less than one. The exponential of the negative fractional divisor weight is in \\(L^a\\) by the crossing-coordinate integral. The conjugate Hölder exponent for the remaining weights is allowed by the fixed-exponent bounds; a second Hölder inequality handles the two endpoint weights in \\(h_{1,k}\\). This proves (3.4).</p><p>The logarithmic sum gives \\(\\Phi_{2,k}\\leq\\Phi_{0,k}\\), hence \\(\\Phi_{1,k}\\leq\\Phi_{0,k}\\). The defining section of \\(E\\) therefore satisfies (3.5), making multiplication a contraction for any one fixed background metric.</p><p>On \\(U\\), divisor weights contribute no curvature. The curvature lower bounds and the displayed convex-combination formula follow from the definitions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2, with the subsequent cutoff construction", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V\\) be a smooth projective complex variety with Kähler form \\(\\omega\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D\\) be a reduced simple-normal-crossing divisor, possibly empty, and put \\(U=V\\setminus D\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a complete Kähler metric \\(\\omega_c\\geq\\omega\\) on \\(U\\) such that, on sufficiently small coordinate neighborhoods in \\(V\\), it has potentials bounded even on approach to \\(D\\).</p><p>If \\(D\\neq\\varnothing\\), there is a smooth proper exhaustion \\(r\\) of \\(U\\) with bounded gradient in \\(\\omega_c\\). Consequently, \\(U\\) has compactly supported smooth cutoffs tending to one with gradients tending uniformly to zero. If \\(D=\\varnothing\\), the constant cutoff one suffices.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(D\\) is empty, take \\(\\omega_c=\\omega\\). Otherwise choose a smooth metric on \\(\\mathcal O_V(D)\\) and scale it so that\n\\[\nt=\\log|s_D|_{\\mathrm{sm}}^2&lt;-e^2\n\\quad\\text{on }U.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nf_0(t)=\\frac1{\\log(-t)}.\n\\]\nFor \\(x=-t\\),\n\\[\nf_0'(t)=\\frac1{x(\\log x)^2},\n\\qquad\nf_0''(t)=\\frac1{x^2}\n\\left(\\frac1{(\\log x)^2}+\\frac2{(\\log x)^3}\\right).\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(f_0'\\) is bounded and\n\\[\nf_0''(t)\\geq(t\\log(-t))^{-2}.\n\\]\nOff \\(D\\), \\(i\\partial\\bar\\partial t\\) is the negative of a fixed smooth curvature form, so it is bounded by a multiple of \\(\\omega\\). For a sufficiently large fixed \\(M\\),\n\\[\n\\omega_c=M\\omega+i\\partial\\bar\\partial f_0(t)\n\\]\nis positive and dominates both \\(\\omega\\) and\n\\[\n\\frac{i\\partial t\\wedge\\bar\\partial t}{(t\\log(-t))^2}.\n\\]</p><p>A path leaving every compact subset of \\(U\\) approaches \\(D\\), because \\(V\\) is compact; along such an approach \\(t\\to-\\infty\\). The radial term bounds its length below by a positive constant times the total variation of \\(\\log\\log(-t)\\), which diverges. Hence the metric is complete, including at crossings.</p><p>If \\(\\omega=i\\partial\\bar\\partial\\rho\\) on a small coordinate neighborhood, then\n\\[\nM\\rho+f_0(t)\n\\]\nis a local potential for \\(\\omega_c\\). The smooth \\(\\rho\\) is bounded on a smaller relatively compact neighborhood, and \\(f_0(t)\\) is bounded and tends to zero at \\(D\\).</p><p>For \\(D\\neq\\varnothing\\), the function\n\\[\nr=\\log\\log(-t)\n\\]\nis a smooth proper exhaustion with bounded gradient in \\(\\omega_c\\). If \\(\\zeta\\) is smooth, equal to one on \\((-\\infty,1]\\) and zero on \\([2,\\infty)\\), then \\(\\zeta(r/R)\\), as \\(R\\to\\infty\\), gives the required cutoffs, with gradient \\(O(R^{-1})\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Proposition 3.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V\\) be a smooth projective complex variety of dimension \\(n&gt;0\\), let \\(D\\) be a reduced simple-normal-crossing divisor, and put \\(U=V\\setminus D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix the complete Kähler metric \\(\\omega_c\\geq\\omega\\) with bounded local potentials furnished by Lemma 6.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F\\) be a fixed holomorphic line bundle on \\(V\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(h_k\\) be smooth metric weights on \\(F|_U\\), and suppose that for one fixed \\(A_0\\geq0\\),\n  \\[\n  i\\partial\\bar\\partial h_k\\geq-A_0\\omega_c.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">In local frames on \\(V\\), assume that the \\(h_k\\) are uniformly bounded above and the functions \\(e^{-h_k}\\) have uniformly bounded local integrals on punctured coordinate neighborhoods.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(L^2_{h_k}(n,q)\\) for the Hilbert space of \\(F\\)-valued \\((n,q)\\)-forms using \\(h_k\\) and \\(\\omega_c\\), take the maximal distributional realization of \\(\\bar\\partial\\), and put\n  \\[\n  Z^1_{h_k}\n  =\\ker\\!\\left(\\bar\\partial:L^2_{h_k}(n,1)\\to L^2_{h_k}(n,2)\\right).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. There is a continuous linear map\n   \\[\n   \\kappa_k:Z^1_{h_k}\\longrightarrow H^1(V,K_V\\otimes F)\n   \\]\n   agreeing with the ordinary Dolbeault class on smooth closed forms on \\(V\\). It annihilates the closure in \\(L^2_{h_k}(n,1)\\) of the image of\n   \\[\n   \\bar\\partial:L^2_{h_k}(n,0)\\to L^2_{h_k}(n,1).\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. There is a constant \\(C\\), independent of \\(k\\), such that every \\(f\\in Z^1_{h_k}\\) with \\(\\kappa_k(f)=0\\) has a global primitive on \\(U\\) satisfying\n   \\[\n   \\bar\\partial v=f,\\qquad \\|v\\|_{h_k}\\leq C\\|f\\|_{h_k}.\n   \\]\n   No continuous linear choice of these global primitives is asserted.</p><p>In particular,\n\\[\n\\ker\\kappa_k=\\operatorname{im}\\bar\\partial\n=\\overline{\\operatorname{im}\\bar\\partial}\n\\quad\\text{inside }Z^1_{h_k}.\n\\]\nThe proposition also applies to a fixed smooth line metric on \\(F\\) over all of \\(V\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 7.1 and 7.2 construct the continuous ordinary class map and prove that it annihilates the closure of exact forms. Lemma 7.3 supplies a uniformly bounded primitive for every form in its kernel. Therefore\n\\[\n\\ker\\kappa_k=\\operatorname{im}\\bar\\partial\n=\\overline{\\operatorname{im}\\bar\\partial}.\n\\]</p><p>Both sequences \\(h_{0,k},h_{1,k}\\) in Proposition 6 satisfy these hypotheses by (3.4) and (3.6).</p><p>For a fixed smooth line metric from \\(V\\), its curvature is bounded below by \\(-A_0\\omega_c\\) for some \\(A_0\\), and its local upper and integral bounds are automatic. Thus the proposition applies to such a fixed metric as well.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Uniform local solution operators.", "statement": "<p>Uniform local solution operators.</p><p>Under the assumptions of Proposition 7, choose a finite collection of coordinate and frame balls\n\\[\nV_j\\Subset U_j\\Subset\\widehat U_j\n\\]\nsuch that the \\(V_j\\) cover \\(V\\) and \\(\\omega_c\\) has a bounded local potential \\(\\rho_j\\) on \\(\\widehat U_j\\cap U\\). These choices are independent of \\(k\\).</p><p>For every \\(j,k\\), there is a continuous linear local solution operator \\(T_{j,k}\\) on closed \\(L^2_{h_k}\\) data on \\(U_j\\cap U\\) such that\n\\[\n\\bar\\partial T_{j,k}f=f,\n\\qquad\n\\|T_{j,k}f\\|_{h_k,U_j\\cap U}\n\\leq C_j\\|f\\|_{h_k,U_j\\cap U},\n\\tag{3.7}\n\\]\nwhere \\(C_j\\) is independent of \\(k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>On \\(U_j\\cap U\\), replace the line weight by\n\\[\nh_k+(A_0+1)\\rho_j.\n\\]\nIts curvature dominates \\(\\omega_c\\), and the bounded potential compares its top-form norms with those of \\(h_k\\) by fixed constants.</p><p>Equip \\(U_j\\cap U\\) with the complete metric\n\\[\n\\omega_c+\\omega_{\\mathrm{ball}},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\omega_{\\mathrm{ball}}\\) is a complete metric for the coordinate ball. The sum is complete at both the deleted divisor and the ball boundary. An \\((n,1)\\)-datum square integrable for \\(\\omega_c\\) remains so for the larger background metric: its density uses the inverse background matrix on its remaining covector.</p><p>By Proposition 1, the inverse-curvature density for the modified weight is bounded by the original \\(\\omega_c\\)-datum density. The top-form solution density is independent of the auxiliary complete metric. This gives (3.7) with \\(C_j\\) independent of \\(k\\).</p><p>For each fixed \\(k\\), choose the solution of smallest norm in the modified metric. Orthogonal projection onto the complement of the holomorphic kernel makes this a linear continuous choice. The affine solution set is closed because the distributional operator is closed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Extended holomorphic cocycles and the ordinary class map.", "statement": "<p>Extended holomorphic cocycles and the ordinary class map.</p><p>Retain Proposition 7 and the cover and local solution operators of Lemma 7.1. For \\(f\\in Z^1_{h_k}\\), put\n\\[\nw_j=T_{j,k}(f|_{U_j\\cap U}).\n\\]\nThen \\(w_j-w_i\\) extends uniquely across \\(D\\) to a holomorphic section\n\\[\nc_{ij}\\in H^0(U_i\\cap U_j,K_V\\otimes F),\n\\]\nand the \\(c_{ij}\\) form a Čech cocycle.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal Z^1\\) be the Fréchet space of holomorphic cocycles on this fixed finite cover, with compact-convergence topology. There is a continuous linear map\n\\[\n\\kappa:\\mathcal Z^1\\to H^1(V,K_V\\otimes F)\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n\\kappa_k(f)=\\kappa((c_{ij}))\n\\]\nhas the properties in part 1 of Proposition 7.</p><p>Moreover, the cocycles associated to all \\(k\\) and all \\(f\\) with bounded \\(\\|f\\|_{h_k}\\) are bounded in every fixed compact-convergence seminorm, uniformly in \\(k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>On \\((U_i\\cap U_j)\\cap U\\), the difference \\(w_j-w_i\\) is holomorphic. Uniform upper bounds for \\(h_k\\) turn (3.7) into ordinary local \\(L^2\\) bounds in frames on \\(V\\). Holomorphic \\(L^2\\) removal therefore extends the differences uniquely across \\(D\\). Locally, Laurent expansion in crossing coordinates and Fubini show that a nonzero negative power is not square integrable. The extended differences form a holomorphic Čech cocycle.</p><p>For any compact set in an overlap, choose a slightly larger compact neighborhood still in that overlap. The ordinary \\(L^2\\) bound there and the holomorphic mean-value inequality bound the supremum on the smaller compact. Each compact has positive separation from the ball boundaries. Its constant may depend on the compact, but not on \\(k\\). No bound on the supremum over an entire open overlap is required.</p><p>The cocycle equations make \\(\\mathcal Z^1\\) a closed subspace of the finite product of overlap section spaces. Choose a smooth partition of unity \\((\\eta_i)\\) with\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Supp}\\eta_i\\Subset U_i.\n\\]\nFor a cocycle \\(c\\), put\n\\[\nb_j=\\sum_i\\eta_i c_{ij}\\quad\\text{on }U_j,\n\\]\nextending each summand by zero outside its overlap. Then \\(b_j-b_i=c_{ij}\\), so the forms \\(\\bar\\partial b_j\\) agree and define a global smooth form \\(\\beta_c\\). Define\n\\[\n\\kappa(c)=[\\beta_c].\n\\]\nContinuity follows from the compact support of the partition and Cauchy estimates for derivatives on slightly larger compact sets; ordinary cohomology is finite-dimensional and Hausdorff.</p><p>Changing the local primitives changes the cocycle by a holomorphic coboundary: differences of the primitives extend by the same ordinary \\(L^2\\) argument. The local solution estimates and compact-seminorm estimates therefore make \\(\\kappa_k\\) continuous and linear.</p><p>If \\(f\\) is smooth on \\(V\\), choose smooth local primitives on the larger balls. On the smaller balls they have finite weighted norms because their coefficients are bounded and the local integrals of \\(e^{-h_k}\\) are finite. Comparison with these primitives shows that \\(\\kappa_k\\) agrees with the ordinary Dolbeault class.</p><p>If \\(f=\\bar\\partial v\\) for a global weighted \\(L^2\\) primitive on \\(U\\), then \\(w_j-v\\) is holomorphic on \\(U_j\\cap U\\) and ordinary locally \\(L^2\\). Its extension gives a holomorphic splitting of \\(c_{ij}\\), so \\(\\kappa_k(f)=0\\). Continuity therefore annihilates the closure of the actual exact forms.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A uniformly bounded splitting and global primitive.", "statement": "<p>A uniformly bounded splitting and global primitive.</p><p>Under Proposition 7 and Lemma 7.2, put\n\\[\n\\mathcal C^0=\\prod_jH^0(U_j,K_V\\otimes F),\n\\]\nwith compact-convergence topology, let\n\\[\n\\delta:\\mathcal C^0\\to\\mathcal Z^1\n\\]\nbe the coboundary map, and set \\(\\mathcal B^1=\\delta(\\mathcal C^0)\\). Then\n\\[\n\\mathcal B^1=\\ker\\kappa.\n\\tag{3.8}\n\\]</p><p>For cocycles arising from all \\(k\\) and all \\(f\\in Z^1_{h_k}\\) with \\(\\|f\\|_{h_k}\\leq1\\) and \\(\\kappa_k(f)=0\\), there are holomorphic splittings \\((a_j)\\) with\n\\[\n\\max_j\\sup_{\\overline{V_j}}|a_j|\\leq C_{\\mathrm{split}},\n\\]\nwhere norms use fixed smooth frames or a fixed smooth bundle metric, and \\(C_{\\mathrm{split}}\\) is independent of \\(k,f\\).</p><p>Consequently, every \\(f\\in\\ker\\kappa_k\\) has a global primitive \\(v\\) on \\(U\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\|v\\|_{h_k}\\leq C\\|f\\|_{h_k}\n\\]\nwith \\(C\\) independent of \\(k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The inclusion \\(\\mathcal B^1\\subseteq\\ker\\kappa\\) is immediate. For the reverse inclusion, construct smooth local cochains from a partition of unity for a cocycle in \\(\\ker\\kappa\\). Their common \\(\\bar\\partial\\) is a global smooth form with zero ordinary Dolbeault class. Subtract a global smooth primitive from every cochain. The resulting cochains are holomorphic and split the original cocycle. This proves (3.8) without assuming that the coordinate cover is Leray.</p><p>Thus \\(\\mathcal B^1\\) is a closed Fréchet subspace. The continuous surjection\n\\[\n\\delta:\\mathcal C^0\\to\\mathcal B^1\n\\]\nis open. Prescribe only the single continuous seminorm\n\\[\nq((a_j))=\\max_j\\sup_{\\overline{V_j}}|a_j|.\n\\]\nOpenness says that the image of \\(\\{q&lt;1\\}\\) contains a zero neighborhood of \\(\\mathcal B^1\\) described by finitely many compact seminorms. By Lemma 7.2, cocycles arising from all \\(k\\) and all data of norm at most one are bounded in those seminorms. A single rescaling therefore gives, for every such cocycle in \\(\\mathcal B^1\\), a splitting with \\(q((a_j))\\leq C_{\\mathrm{split}}\\). This uses no assertion that a bounded subset has a lift bounded in every Fréchet seminorm.</p><p>When \\(\\kappa_k(f)=0\\), choose this splitting and set\n\\[\nv=w_j-a_j\\quad\\text{on }U_j\\cap U.\n\\]\nThe expressions agree on full overlaps and define a global primitive on \\(U\\). Estimate its norm on the smaller \\(V_j\\), which cover \\(V\\). The local primitives are controlled by (3.7); the corrections are controlled by their supremum on \\(\\overline{V_j}\\) and the uniform integral of \\(e^{-h_k}\\) there. Summing over the finite cover gives a uniform bound for unit-norm data. Scaling proves the assertion for arbitrary \\(f\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Harmonic representatives and the Bochner comparison.", "statement": "<p>Harmonic representatives and the Bochner comparison.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the assumptions of Theorem 2 with \\(n=\\dim V&gt;0\\), and use the weights, complete metric, divisor \\(E\\), and multiplier \\(s:L_1\\to L_0\\) of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\gamma\\) be a smooth closed \\(L_1\\)-valued \\((n,1)\\)-form on \\(V\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All norms use \\(\\omega_c\\); a subscript \\(i,k\\) indicates the line weight \\(h_{i,k}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\Lambda=\\Lambda_{\\omega_c}\\), let \\(D'_{i,k}\\) be the \\((1,0)\\) Chern derivative, and put, in bidegree \\((n,1)\\),\n  \\[\n  A_{i,k}=[\\theta_{i,k}+\\epsilon_k\\omega_c,\\Lambda].\n  \\]\n  These are nonnegative operators, and\n  \\[\n  A_{1,k}\\geq(1-\\lambda)A_{0,k}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The norms \\(\\|\\gamma\\|_{1,k}\\) are uniformly bounded, and there are smooth forms \\(u_k\\) on \\(U\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\gamma-u_k\\in\\overline{\\operatorname{im}\\bar\\partial},\n\\quad\n\\bar\\partial u_k=0,\n\\quad\n\\bar\\partial_{1,k}^*u_k=0,\n\\quad\n\\|u_k\\|_{1,k}\\leq\\|\\gamma\\|_{1,k},\n\\tag{3.9}\n\\]\nwhere the closure is taken in the source weighted degree-one space.</p><p>They satisfy\n\\[\n\\|D_{1,k}'{}^*u_k\\|_{1,k}^2\n+\\int_U\\langle A_{1,k}u_k,u_k\\rangle_{1,k}\\,dV_{\\omega_c}\n\\leq\\epsilon_k\\|u_k\\|_{1,k}^2,\n\\tag{3.11}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\|D_{0,k}'{}^*(su_k)\\|_{0,k}^2\n+\\int_U\\langle A_{0,k}su_k,su_k\\rangle_{0,k}\\,dV_{\\omega_c}\n\\leq C_\\lambda\\epsilon_k\\|u_k\\|_{1,k}^2,\n\\tag{3.12}\n\\]\nwhere \\(C_\\lambda=(1-\\lambda)^{-1}\\).</p><p>Moreover, \\(su_k\\) belongs to the domain of the target Hilbert adjoint and\n\\[\n\\|\\bar\\partial_{0,k}^*(su_k)\\|_{0,k}\\leq C\\sqrt{\\epsilon_k}\n\\tag{3.13}\n\\]\nfor a constant \\(C\\) independent of \\(k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 8.1 gives the harmonic representatives, uniform norms, and source energy estimate. Lemma 8.2 transfers the energy through the contraction and curvature comparison and establishes the target adjoint estimate and domain statement.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "harmonic representatives and their energy", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 8, orthogonal projection away from the closure of the image of degree-zero forms in the maximal \\(L^2_{1,k}\\) Dolbeault complex gives \\(u_k\\) satisfying (3.9).", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 8, orthogonal projection away from the closure of the image of degree-zero forms in the maximal \\(L^2_{1,k}\\) Dolbeault complex gives \\(u_k\\) satisfying (3.9). These forms are smooth on \\(U\\), their norms are uniformly bounded, and they satisfy (3.11).</p>", "proof": "<p>Enlarging \\(\\omega\\) to \\(\\omega_c\\) decreases the top-form-valued \\((0,1)\\)-density. The coefficients of \\(\\gamma\\) are bounded on a finite cover, where (3.4) applies. Hence \\(\\|\\gamma\\|_{1,k}\\) is uniformly bounded.</p><p>In the maximal weighted Dolbeault complex, the closure of the degree-zero image lies in the closed kernel of the degree-one operator. Subtract its orthogonal projection from \\(\\gamma\\), obtaining \\(u_k\\). This gives (3.9). The adjoint assertion follows from orthogonality to the actual range and does not require that range to be closed. Local elliptic regularity for the smooth weighted Dolbeault Laplacian makes \\(u_k\\) smooth on \\(U\\).</p><p>The smooth compact-support Bochner–Kodaira identity is\n\\[\n\\|\\bar\\partial v\\|_{i,k}^2+\\|\\bar\\partial_{i,k}^*v\\|_{i,k}^2\n=\n\\|D_{i,k}'{}^*v\\|_{i,k}^2\n+\\int_U\\langle[\\theta_{i,k},\\Lambda]v,v\\rangle_{i,k}\n\\,dV_{\\omega_c}.\n\\tag{3.10}\n\\]\nHere \\(D'_{i,k}v=0\\) in top holomorphic degree, and \\([\\omega_c,\\Lambda]\\) is the identity in bidegree \\((n,1)\\). The corresponding complete-manifold identity and domain interpretation are discussed in [19, Proposition 2.4].</p><p>To use the identity without already knowing that the extra terms are integrable, take the compactly supported smooth cutoffs from Proposition 6, whose gradients tend uniformly to zero. Apply (3.10) to cutoff multiples of \\(u_k\\) and add \\(\\epsilon_k\\) times their squared norms. The derivative terms on the left tend to zero by (3.9) and the gradient bound. The two terms retained on the right are nonnegative, so lower semicontinuity gives (3.11).</p><p>The commutators with the cutoffs contain only their derivatives; they introduce no constants from derivatives of the varying line metrics.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Target energy and the adjoint-domain conclusion.", "statement": "<p>Target energy and the adjoint-domain conclusion.</p><p>In the setting of Proposition 8, let \\(u_k\\) be the forms supplied by Lemma 8.1. Then \\(su_k\\) is smooth, closed, square integrable in the target space, satisfies (3.12), belongs to the target Hilbert-adjoint domain, and satisfies (3.13).</p>", "proof": "<p>The Kähler identity\n\\[\nD'{}^*=i[\\Lambda,\\bar\\partial]\n\\]\nshows that this formal operator commutes with holomorphic multiplication by \\(s\\). Combining the contraction (3.5), the curvature comparison (3.6), and (3.11) gives (3.12).</p><p>For the curvature term, first use\n\\[\nA_{1,k}\\geq(1-\\lambda)A_{0,k}\n\\]\nand then the line-norm contraction. The constant\n\\[\nC_\\lambda=(1-\\lambda)^{-1}\n\\]\nbounds both the derivative term and the curvature term in the source energy. The strict interior hypothesis keeps this factor finite.</p><p>The form \\(su_k\\) is smooth, closed, and square integrable. Apply the compact-support identity to its cutoff multiples. On the right, use (3.12), replace the curvature commutator by the larger nonnegative \\(A_{0,k}\\), and let gradient errors tend to zero. It follows that the formal adjoint is square integrable and satisfies (3.13), since the \\(\\|u_k\\|_{1,k}\\) are uniformly bounded.</p><p>This also verifies the Hilbert-adjoint domain condition. Complete-metric cutoffs and local smooth approximation give density in the maximal graph norm, so integration by parts against compactly supported forms extends to that domain. The formal adjoint, now known to be \\(L^2\\), is its Hilbert adjoint. Thus (3.13) assumes neither a global primitive nor an adjoint-domain statement in advance.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Theorem 4.2, “Lazić–Peternell, fourfold nonvanishing.” This is a published input", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For a \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier divisor \\(P\\) on a normal projective variety \\(X\\), say that \\(P\\) satisfies the metric condition of [17, Definition 2.12] if there are a positive integer \\(r\\), with \\(rP\\) Cartier, and a resolution \\(f:Z\\to X\\) carrying a singular metric on \\(f^*\\mathcal O_X(rP)\\) whose curvature has the form\n  \\[\n  T=T_0+\\sum_j a_j[E_j],\n  \\qquad\n  T_0\\geq0,\\quad\n  \\nu(T_0,z)=0\\ (z\\in Z),\\quad\n  a_j\\in\\mathbb Q_{\\geq0}.\n  \\tag{4.1}\n  \\]\n  The divisor sum is finite and may be zero. Such a metric has generalized algebraic singularities.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((X,\\Delta)\\) be a normal projective complex klt pair of dimension four, with effective rational boundary, and put \\(D=K_X+\\Delta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(D\\) is pseudoeffective, \\(N\\) is a nef \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-Cartier divisor, both \\(D\\) and \\(D+N\\) satisfy (4.1), and \\(\\chi(X,\\mathcal O_X)\\neq0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a rational \\(t_0&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\kappa(X,D+tN)\\geq0\n\\quad\\text{for every rational }t\\in[0,t_0].\n\\]\nThe interval includes \\(t=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>This is [17, Corollary D], the unconditional four-dimensional consequence of their Theorem C(i). It is used as a published nonvanishing criterion; the paper does not reproduce its proof.</p><p>A semipositive metric with zero Lelong numbers everywhere satisfies (4.1) with zero divisor sum.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Theorem 4.3, “Gongyo–Matsumura, fourfold semiampleness.” This is a published input", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((X,\\Delta)\\) be a normal projective complex klt pair of dimension four, with effective rational boundary, and put \\(D=K_X+\\Delta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that, for a projective birational morphism \\(f:Z\\to X\\) with \\(Z\\) smooth and some positive integer \\(r\\) with \\(rD\\) Cartier, the actual bundle\n  \\[\n  f^*\\mathcal O_X(rD)\n  \\]\n  carries a semipositive singular Hermitian metric with zero Lelong numbers at every point of \\(Z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(\\kappa(X,D)\\geq0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The divisor \\(D\\) is semiample.</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the form of [11, Corollary 5.3] used in the paper. Positivity of the Cartier multiple is also explicit in their Theorem 5.1, from which that corollary follows. The result is used as a published semiampleness criterion; its proof is not reproduced.</p><p>Neither this criterion nor Proposition 9 requires \\(X\\) to be \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-factorial or non-uniruled. Their dimension-three inputs are already incorporated in the stated corollaries.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [0, 3, "cite"], [1, 3, "cite"], [4, 8, "cite"], [4, 18, "cite"], [5, 0, "cite"], [5, 9, "cite"], [6, 5, "cite"], [7, 5, "cite"], [8, 11, "cite"], [9, 0, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [11, 9, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [12, 9, "cite"], [13, 1, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [13, 15, "cite"], [14, 1, "cite"], [14, 17, "cite"], [14, 21, "cite"], [14, 22, "cite"], [15, 14, "cite"], [16, 14, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [17, 1, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [19, 17, "cite"], [19, 20, "cite"], [20, 17, "cite"], [21, 1, "cite"], [22, 21, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [23, 21, "cite"], [24, 3, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [25, 3, "cite"]]});
